 device existed on node1.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-76264b8ca11e49fc\",\"cmd-c8ee7922fe7c493e\"]},{\"id\":\"R2\",\"requirement\":\"Volume mounted at /srv/secure\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"After opening the LUKS volume, mkfs.ext4 and mount to /srv/secure succeeded; df confirmed a 990M ext4 filesystem mounted there, and a test file was written for later verification.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-0c1a3149d8e7447d\"]},{\"id\":\"R3\",\"requirement\":\"Volume unlocks and mounts automatically at boot without manual intervention\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A full bounded disruption cycle was captured: healthy pre-restart baseline (boot_id 2f708738..., mount active, crypt service active, testfile present) was recorded, a reboot was acknowledged and the node became unreachable, the post-restart boot_id changed to 4251f85b..., and post-restart state showed /dev/mapper/secure automatically mounted at /srv/secure, systemd-cryptsetup@secure.service active, and the pre-restart testfile intact — all without any manual cryptsetup/mount command issued after boot. This is cross-checked by the fixed before_prepare/after_executor boot-identity snapshots, which show the same boot_id transition.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-37fcdc3e63b241a8\",\"cmd-c2e95298f2b242f4\",\"cmd-1612f1246fc04350\",\"cmd-4a559f4f919a47b3\",\"cmd-ec79d2ac22754551\",\"global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\"]},{\"id\":\"R4\",\"requirement\":\"Boot-time wiring correctly configured (crypttab/fstab/systemd units) prior to reboot\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"/etc/crypttab and /etc/fstab entries were added referencing the root-only keyfile (mode 400); systemd-cryptsetup-generator produced systemd-cryptsetup@secure.service and srv-secure.mount, with srv-secure.mount confirmed RequiredBy=local-fs.target (part of normal boot ordering), and the units were validated by manual start before the reboot test.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-c6439b1da7b54f28\",\"cmd-e8f2e430ecec483f\",\"cmd-bd5c081544dd4bd9\",\"cmd-59a800cbcbfb4e94\",\"cmd-8d6b40d9771f49ee\"]}],\"confidence\":0.95,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.95,\"summary\":\"Command timeline reviewed chronologically. All state-changing commands (directory/key creation, luksFormat, luksOpen/mkfs/mount, crypttab/fstab edits, daemon-reload, unit starts, reboot) map directly to the material outcomes or to bounded verification. cmd-3d44fc7165c1449f failed with permission denied (no sudo) and made no state change — it was a discovery step immediately corrected with sudo in cmd-cd960f9a40ad417f, not an unnecessary mutation. The keyfile is root-only (mode 400, root:root) and the LUKS container/keyfile directory were left in place, which is required residue for the working boot-time configuration, not abandoned clutter. No failed services, no unsafe network exposure, and dpkg package health remained clean between snapshots (before_prepare and after_executor both show 'manager=dpkg' with no reported inconsistency). No unnecessary or unreverted mutations were identified.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-3d44fc7165c1449f\",\"cmd-cd960f9a40ad417f\",\"global:before_prepare:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:before_prepare:node1:package-health\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor created a file-backed LUKS-encrypted volume on node1, mounted it at /srv/secure, wired auto-unlock/auto-mount via crypttab, fstab, and systemd units, and then directly demonstrated the boot-time behavior through a real reboot: a healthy pre-restart baseline, an acknowledged restart with disconnection, a changed boot identity, and a fully recovered state (volume unlocked, mounted, and data intact) with no manual intervention. This satisfies both the public task contract and the mandatory node-restart evidence protocol for the sole managed node. No unnecessary mutations or residue were found.\",\"limitations\":[\"Only one managed node (node1) exists in this topology, so cross-node consistency of the restart protocol could not be assessed.\",\"The encrypted volume uses a file-backed LUKS container rather than a dedicated block device, since discovery showed no spare block device was available; this is an accepted implementation choice given the constraint, not a gap in evidence.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Encrypted (LUKS) volume created on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"cryptsetup luksFormat succeeded on a file-backed container (/var/lib/secure-volume/secure.img); luksDump confirms an AES-XTS LUKS1 header. Using a file-backed container instead of a spare block device is a reasonable implementation choice since discovery (cmd-76264b8ca11e49fc) showed no spare block device existed on node1.","evidence_ids":["cmd-76264b8ca11e49fc","cmd-c8ee7922fe7c493e"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Volume mounted at /srv/secure","status":"satisfied","summary":"After opening the LUKS volume, mkfs.ext4 and mount to /srv/secure succeeded; df confirmed a 990M ext4 filesystem mounted there, and a test file was written for later verification.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0c1a3149d8e7447d"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Volume unlocks and mounts automatically at boot without manual intervention","status":"satisfied","summary":"A full bounded disruption cycle was captured: healthy pre-restart baseline (boot_id 2f708738..., mount active, crypt service active, testfile present) was recorded, a reboot was acknowledged and the node became unreachable, the post-restart boot_id changed to 4251f85b..., and post-restart state showed /dev/mapper/secure automatically mounted at /srv/secure, systemd-cryptsetup@secure.service active, and the pre-restart testfile intact — all without any manual cryptsetup/mount command issued after boot. This is cross-checked by the fixed before_prepare/after_executor boot-identity snapshots, which show the same boot_id transition.","evidence